    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Written mostly in hiragana, the script then associated with women's writing, it follows Genji's loves, exile and return at court and then two of his descendants, through 54 chapters and some four hundred characters who age in step.</li><li>The original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> is lost and its authorship debated; the 13th-century editions of Minamoto no Chikayuki and Fujiwara Teika became the basis of most later copies.</li><li>Often called the first novel, a claim much debated, it is staple reading in Japanese schools, appears on the 2000 yen banknote and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> its Classics Day, 1 November.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> game Genjikō, players matched the scents of five incense samples without being told their names, and each possible combination had a symbol, a genji-mon, standing for a chapter of the tale.</p></aside>
    </section>

